World Wide Web Inventor Plans to Start New Internet Retrolunch for Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, has launched a start-up attempt to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The technology legend's latest project, Inrupt, is a company based on Berners-Lee's open-source Solid platform.
Solid allows users to decide where their data is stored, and who has access to any information.
In an interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee jot jokes Inrupt's intention to "dominate the world."
"You have to do it right now," he said, citing his start-up company.
"It's a historic moment."
The app uses Solid's technology to allow people to create their own "personal repository," or POD.
It may include contact lists, checklists, a calendar, a music library and other personal and professional tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify all available in a single browser at the same time.
What’s unique in a personal repository is that it is entirely up to the user who has access to what type of data.
The company calls it "personal empowerment through information."
According to the company's chief executive of John Bruce, Inrupti's idea is to provide resources, processes and the required skills to bring Solid to everyone.
The company currently comprises a security platform purchased by Berners-Lee, Bruce, IBM, hired developers working with the project, as well as a community of voluntary coddlers.
Technological developers around the world will be able to create their own decentralized applications with tools available on Inrupt's website.
Berners-Lee says he and his team won't discuss "with Facebook and Google whether to offer people a complete change where all their business models are turned on their heads overnight."
“We don’t ask for their consent.”
Berners-Lee, in an article published in Medium on Saturday, wrote Inrupti's "the mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem for the uniformity and quality of a newly built-built network for Solid."
Berners-Lee revolutionised the Internet by establishing the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
Over the past few months, Berners-Lee has played an influential voice in a debate on net neutrality.
Berners-Lee maintains his position as the founder and manager of the World Wide Web Consortium, Web Foundation and Open Data Institute even after Inrupt's launch.
"I'm extremely optimistic in keeping an eye on the network's next era," Berners-Lee added.
