An environmental organisation eavesdrop on hidden lives of bats
A new study to be carried out in the Scottish Highlands region aims to determine how bats use terrain when hunting for food.
It is hoped a new study will give new light to the behaviour of these unique flying mammals and guidance in future conservation measures.
Research carried out by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland monitors ailing and dwarf-killing, as well as earbetherers and water sips in the Inveverewe Garden of the Botanical Garden in Wester Ross.
In the study, special storage devices will be placed at important points in the area to monitor bat activity during the season.
