Voturing frequencies, as well as which species at any given time, the analysis of experts will be ensured.
On the basis of the results, a habitat map will be created, in which bat behavior can be presented in an image that follows the scale of the area.
Rob Dewar, the organization's Nature Conservation Adviser hopes the results will reveal which areas of habitat are most important for bats, and how different species make use of them.
The information is helpful in the planning of habitat areas, such as the creation of meadows and the management of forest areas, taking into account bats and related species.
Batpopulations in Scotland and the United Kingdom as a whole have declined substantially over the past 100 years.
Building and civil engineering work threaten bats of resting places and habitats.
Wind turbines and illumination may also pose a risk, as well as flypapers and certain chemicals used in building materials, as well as attacks on pet cats.
