It employs ten experts who carry out regular surveys, rest area inspections and, in some cases, animal resettlement operations.
The organization has even set up Scotland's first and only-on-Brother-of-flocal conservation site for the protection of bats on Dumfries and Threave Manor in the Galloway region.
Farm manager David Thompson says the area is ideal for bats.
"Threave suits bats very well," he says.
"We've got old buildings and trees and, overall, a good living environment.
We don't know everything about bats yet, so our work here and other areas of the organization helps us to better understand what bats need to thrive."
He emphasises how important it is to retrieve buildings for bats before renovating, because displacing one bat's lingonberry relenting can inadvertentently cause up to 400 female bludgeony blubbers and the death of a puppet and eradicate an entire local population.
Batteries are protected animals and killing, harassing, disturbing or destroying their places of rest is prohibited.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish clerk of the Bat Conservation Organisation, encourages the public to take part in conservation work.
He says: "We still have a lot to learn about our bats, and we don't yet know how their populations are doing."
