U.S.-Chinese Trade War Can Benefit Beijing
The starting gun for the U.S.-China trade war was deafening and, while the fight is far from over, a rift between the two countries can benefit Beijing in the long term, experts say.
U.S. President Donald Trump issued a first warning earlier this year by imposing import taxes on China's main export articles, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
Significant escalation took place this week with new tariffs that affect imported goods nil tune of nil tune of nil 200 billion (€150bn), effectively taxing half of all imports from China to the United States.
Beijing has responded at the same time, most recently imposing tariffs of five to ten per cent on American imported goods worth bucks of 60 billion dollars.
“Chinese has pledged to respond to the United States with an eye-to-eye style and it doesn't seem that the world's second-biggest economic power is reluctant for a moment.
In order to persuade Washington to back down, one would have to renuncite in its own demands, but public bowing to the U.S. would be far too humiliating for Chinese President Xi Jinping.
Experts, however, note that if Beijing plays its cards correctly, the U.S. trade war could positively affect China in the long-term by reducing interdependencies between the two countries.
"A small political decision, either in Washington or in Beijing, can create the conditions that start an economic sluggish spiral in either country and this is in fact a more dangerous possibility than onlookers have understood until now," said Abigail Grace, a research assistant familiar to Asia at a think-tank of Center for New American Security.
Syria's foreign foreign minister says country is 'prepared to 'premising 'remote' return of refugees
Syria says it's ready for a voluntary re-entry of refugees and calls for help rebuilding the country after more than seven years of destructive war.
Speaking to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said conditions in the country would improve.
"The situation is now more stable and secure thanks to developments in the fight against terrorism," he noted.
The government will continue to fix areas devastated by terrorists to reinstabilize the normal situation.
Circumstancies now make it possible for refugees to re-emerging the country from which they were forced to leave because of unilateral economic activities aimed at their daily lives and livelihoods.
The U.N. estimates more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled the country since the war began in 2011.
In addition, there are still six million people in need of humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moualem stated that the Syrian government would be grateful for the help of a rebuilding of a destructed country.
He stressed, however, that the government would not accept conditional assistance or assistance from states that supported the rebels.
